A complete reference makes the stop and return steps visible:

```python
def sum_list(items, index=0):
    if index == len(items):
        return 0
    return items[index] + sum_list(items, index + 1)
```

For [2,4,6], index 3 returns 0. The paused index-2 call then returns 6+0=6, index 1 returns 4+6=10, and index 0 returns 2+10=12. There is one addition for each item and one active call per suffix, plus the empty base case. Time and call-stack space both grow linearly with list length in this model. Increasing index by one moves toward len(items), so the recursion terminates for the supplied starting index. Python limits recursion depth; use iteration for large lists. An arbitrary external starting index would need its own range contract.
